      "writtenOn": "2022-07-11T16:32:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think making IPv6 (and/or most, if not all, other parts of networking and bpf) a module would be a good idea.  We certainly don\u0027t plan on making the Android (userspace) networking stack functional without IPv6, bpf, cgroups, etc. - we have enough difficulties with just making things work on the existing myriad of configurations without complicating things further by making them optional.\n\nOne of the primary benefits of GKI was supposed to be a reduction in the amount of variability between devices...\n\nThe kernel does not use up a meaningful amount of RAM.  I mean what sort of savings can you get here? maybe around 1 MiB?  Even the weakest Android Go devices have 1 GiB of ram... (indeed with Android T it might have been bumped up to 2 GiB? I think wembley now defaults to 2GB)\n\nUnless we\u0027re planning to ship Android to entirely network-deprived devices?  But not sure what the point of such an exercise would be, as at that point I think you\u0027re probably better off simply not using Android at all.",
      "parentUuid": "baf4dda5_cd096a89",
